New opt-in option `--synonym-refines-abstract-type` (default off; `synonym-refines-abstract-type = true` in a project file): in a refining module, a type synonym `type T<Xs> = U<Xs>` may refine an abstract type `T` that has members, when `U` is a class, trait, datatype, newtype or abstract type declared in the refining module and not in the module it refines; the members of `T` are merged into `U` under the same checks as for any declaration refining `T` (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/91). With the option off nothing changes, and such a synonym is rejected with "a type synonym (T) cannot declare members, so it cannot refine an abstract type with members", as before. With it on, a synonym of another form is rejected with a message that states the required form.
